     <para>
      Add defenses against problems caused by altering composite types
      concurrently with operations on data of those types (Heikki
      Linnakangas, Andres Freund, Nathan Bossart, Nikhil Sontakke)
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;afaff70f0">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;692f4b3c4">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;9ef1cd650">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;9ddd897b3">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;1bb9942e2">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;bcc692493">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;bb66a4e82">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;05fc87f95">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;d48d28c4b">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;67d89bb22">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;e72f4e91c">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;5db4823d0">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;26ff7cbd3">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;fdbb7ffbb">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;ff6d9deca">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;7bc2d46d1">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;dcb2ff56d">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;7c32010ca">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;da2cb6090">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;8b1a2a8ac">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;391884d5c">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;edcd260d9">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;d835dc88d">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;cfdd09589">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;5f0add5cf">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;30d81a0ba">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;edcc027ee">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;412297476">&sect;</ulink>
     </para>

     <para>
      Operations such as <command>ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN
      TYPE</command> are hazardous because tuples of the older row layout
      may still exist in the system.  While we have long had some checks
      to defend against that, they were never really complete, and they
      entirely failed to detect cases where composite types were changed
      concurrently with queries using them.  This voluminous set of
      patches attempts to improve that.
     </para>

     <para>
      Introduce versioning logic to permit detection of composite datums
      that don't match the tuple descriptor we wish to interpret them
      with.  For full protection, this change requires rebuilding
      C-language extensions, and there are some extensions that will
      require source-code changes.  If necessary, the
      new <varname>composite_type_mismatch</varname> server parameter can
      be used to disable these checks, but doing so carries security
      risks.
     </para>

     <para>
      Add locking to prevent cases such as <literal>ALTER TYPE</literal>
      on a composite type used in a <literal>CREATE TABLE</literal> that
      hasn't committed yet.
     </para>

     <para>
      Tighten the rules about whether <literal>ALTER COLUMN TYPE</literal>
      is allowed when dependencies exist on the composite type.  For
      example, it's no longer allowed when a function's default
      expression or new-style SQL function body depends on the type.
     </para>

     <para>
      The <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> Project thanks
      Andrew Gierth, Alexander Lakhin, Francesco Verardi, Hcamael,
      Tomer Fichman, OpenAI Security Research Team, Peter Geoghegan,
      Mehmet Ince, Bryan Green, Sumedh Pathak,
      Sergey Shinderuk, Yuxiao Guo, Nikhil Sontakke, Daniel Bakker,
      the Anthropic OSS program, Claude and Ada Logics
      for reporting this problem.
      (CVE-2026-6460)
     </para>
    </listitem>

    <listitem>
<!--
Author: Tomas Vondra <tomas.vondra@postgresql.org>
Branch: master [3beee6e77] 2026-11-09 06:20:30 -0800
Branch: REL_19_STABLE [2ac7e6e8a] 2026-11-09 06:20:36 -0800
Branch: REL_18_STABLE [4ccea70a1] 2026-11-09 06:20:41 -0800
Branch: REL_17_STABLE [02ca67429] 2026-11-09 06:20:46 -0800
Branch: REL_16_STABLE [71c8a6ac4] 2026-11-09 06:20:51 -0800
Branch: REL_15_STABLE [357a99a4d] 2026-11-09 06:20:55 -0800
Branch: REL_14_STABLE [e8f249272] 2026-11-09 06:21:00 -0800
-->
     <para>
      Cope with concurrent rowtype changes
      in <function>ExecEvalFieldStore[De]Form()</function> (Tomas Vondra,
      Heikki Linnakangas)
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;4ccea70a1">&sect;</ulink>
     </para>

     <para>
      The <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> Project thanks
      OpenAI Codex Security, Bronson Yen, Mehmet Ince,
      Claude and Ada Logics
      for reporting this problem.
      (CVE-2026-116306)
     </para>
    </listitem>

    <listitem>
<!--
Author: Masahiko Sawada <msawada@postgresql.org>
Branch: master [d41c9fad0] 2026-11-09 06:20:30 -0800
Branch: REL_19_STABLE [ee7c8f051] 2026-11-09 06:20:36 -0800
Branch: REL_18_STABLE [780900dcf] 2026-11-09 06:20:41 -0800
-->
     <para>
      Guard against malicious alteration of a table while a statistics
      import function is running on it (Masahiko Sawada)
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;780900dcf">&sect;</ulink>
     </para>

     <para>
      Previously it was possible to store statistics that no longer match
      the table, leading to crashes or other problems later.
     </para>

     <para>
      The <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> Project thanks
      Mehmet Ince
      for reporting this problem.
      (CVE-2026-116329)
     </para>
    </listitem>

    <listitem>
<!--
Author: Peter Geoghegan <pg@bowt.ie>
Branch: master [315c4e4e8] 2026-11-09 06:20:33 -0800
Branch: REL_19_STABLE [253252429] 2026-11-09 06:20:38 -0800
Branch: REL_18_STABLE [a244ef271] 2026-11-09 06:20:43 -0800
Branch: REL_17_STABLE [a14aacebe] 2026-11-09 06:20:48 -0800
Branch: REL_16_STABLE [629f54970] 2026-11-09 06:20:53 -0800
Branch: REL_15_STABLE [8bbd75721] 2026-11-09 06:20:57 -0800
Branch: REL_14_STABLE [4167810cf] 2026-11-09 06:21:01 -0800
-->
     <para>
      Enforce view row visibility in <literal>INSERT ... ON
      CONFLICT</literal> (Peter Geoghegan, Zsolt Parragi)
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;a244ef271">&sect;</ulink>
     </para>

     <para>
      When applied to a view, <literal>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO
      UPDATE</literal> could update rows that should be hidden by the
      view's <literal>WHERE</literal> clause.  Although it was documented,
      this behavior is surely unexpected, and it gives rise to security
      problems: a user can update rows he should have no privilege to
      reach.  Updating is incorrect, but inserting is impossible too (it
      would fail due to the unique arbiter index), so throw an error
      instead when the case arises.  <literal>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO
      SELECT</literal> could show such rows as well, but no longer does.
     </para>

     <para>
      The <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> Project thanks
      Qing Xu, Carl Andersen, Zsolt Parragi, the Anthropic OSS program,
      Shihao Zhong, and Josef Šimánek
      for reporting this problem.
      (CVE-2026-16237)
     </para>
    </listitem>

    <listitem>
<!--
Author: Noah Misch <noah@leadboat.com>
Branch: master [b2f1afd9f] 2026-11-09 06:20:33 -0800
Branch: REL_19_STABLE [5b6f8c974] 2026-11-09 06:20:38 -0800
Branch: REL_18_STABLE [ea228688e] 2026-11-09 06:20:43 -0800
Branch: REL_17_STABLE [0b8d1768f] 2026-11-09 06:20:48 -0800
Branch: REL_16_STABLE [dbc8a36d7] 2026-11-09 06:20:53 -0800
Branch: REL_15_STABLE [98ec97f67] 2026-11-09 06:20:57 -0800
Branch: REL_14_STABLE [b83eab069] 2026-11-09 06:21:01 -0800
-->
     <para>
      Align <literal>REASSIGN OWNED</literal>'s caller-privilege checks
      with <literal>ALTER ... OWNER TO</literal>'s checks (Zsolt Parragi)
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;ea228688e">&sect;</ulink>
     </para>

     <para>
      Since v16, giving an object to another role requires the ability
      to <literal>SET ROLE</literal> to that role.  However, a role
      granted <literal>INHERIT TRUE, SET FALSE</literal> could bypass that
      via <literal>REASSIGN OWNED</literal>.  A role
      granted <literal>INHERIT FALSE, SET TRUE</literal> saw the inverse
      problem: <literal>REASSIGN OWNED</literal> failed
      while <literal>ALTER OWNER</literal> succeeded.
      Make <literal>REASSIGN</literal> follow the <literal>ALTER
      OWNER</literal> rules.
     </para>

     <para>
      <literal>REASSIGN OWNED</literal> also bypassed
      the <literal>CREATEDB</literal> privilege check in <literal>ALTER
      DATABASE OWNER</literal>.
     </para>

     <para>
      In v15 and v14, <literal>REASSIGN OWNED</literal>'s privilege check
      is stricter than the membership checks in <literal>ALTER
      OWNER</literal>.  Leave that alone, but still fix the
      missing <literal>CREATEDB</literal> check.
     </para>

     <para>
      The <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> Project thanks
      Zsolt Parragi
      for reporting this problem.
      (CVE-2026-120146)
     </para>
    </listitem>

    <listitem>
<!--
Author: Masahiko Sawada <msawada@postgresql.org>
Branch: master [4d93c0384] 2026-11-09 06:20:29 -0800
Branch: REL_19_STABLE [8b3babb6e] 2026-11-09 06:20:35 -0800
Branch: REL_18_STABLE [8d9f203b8] 2026-11-09 06:20:40 -0800
Branch: REL_17_STABLE [3094cdb7d] 2026-11-09 06:20:46 -0800
-->
     <para>
      Fix use-after-free in <literal>COPY FROM ON_ERROR</literal>
      reporting (Masahiko Sawada)
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;8d9f203b8">&sect;</ulink>
     </para>

     <para>
      An error report raised in <literal>COPY FROM</literal> could
      disclose server memory.
     </para>

     <para>
      The <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> Project thanks
      Jiyong Ahn, Claude and Ada Logics
      for reporting this problem.
      (CVE-2026-116310)
     </para>
    </listitem>

    <listitem>
<!--
Author: Michael Paquier <michael@paquier.xyz>
Branch: master [5b23669ea] 2026-11-09 06:20:31 -0800
