     <para>
      Perform join removal by editing the query's parse tree, rather than
      trying to fix up the planner's derived data structures (Tom Lane,
      Richard Guo, Shihao Zhong, Ayush Tiwari)
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;9f25197bf">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;37c2bc950">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;476b9eb3c">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;18aca7257">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;e509be9ce">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;37b6e2657">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;83bd1c1ba">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;051db7737">&sect;</ulink>
      <ulink url="&commit_baseurl;3bfc7c849">&sect;</ulink>
     </para>

     <para>
      The original implementation of join removal in the planner has had
      numerous bugs over the years, including some that were never fixed.
      It was inherently fragile because changes in the planner's derived
      data structures often required adjustments in join removal, yet
      there was no easy way to keep that code in sync.  Replace it with
      code that modifies the far-simpler parse tree representation and
      then loops around to re-generate all of the derived data.
      This is a significant change that normally we'd never consider
      back-patching, but the prospect of continuing bugs from the old
      implementation has driven us to take the risk of doing so.
     </para>
    </listitem>

